# imgutil(1) -- Work with confluent OS cloning and diskless images

## SYNOPSIS

`imgutil` `build` [<options>] <scratchdir>  
`imgutil` `build` `--containerfile` [<options>] <file>  
`imgutil` `exec` [<options>] <scratchdir> [<cmd>...]  
`imgutil` `unpack` <profilename> <scratchdir>  
`imgutil` `pack` [<options>] <scratchdir> <profilename>  
`imgutil` `capture` <node> <profilename>

## DESCRIPTION

**imgutil** is a utility for creating, managing, and deploying OS images for diskless boot and system cloning in a Confluent environment. It supports building images from scratch, capturing images from running systems, and packing/unpacking diskless profiles.

## COMMANDS

* `build`:
  Build a new diskless image from scratch in the specified scratch directory.
  With a container image as `-s` source, the image is extracted to the
  scratch directory instead, and completed with the confluent initramfs
  configuration, the package list and a regenerated initramfs. This
  builds images made elsewhere, for example in CI. With
  `--containerfile`, a Containerfile to start such an image from is
  written instead.

* `exec`:
  Start the specified scratch directory as a container and optionally run a command inside it.
  While it runs, `/proc`, `/sys`, `/dev`, an empty `/run` and the build
  system's `/etc/resolv.conf` are mounted, without changing the scratch
  directory. A plain `chroot` lacks them (no DNS, no `/dev/null`), so run
  chroot based tools through `imgutil exec`, see EXAMPLES.

* `unpack`:
  Unpack a diskless image profile to a scratch directory for modification.

* `pack`:
  Pack a scratch directory into a diskless profile that can be deployed.
  If the image enables SELinux in `/etc/selinux/config`, the scratch
  directory is first labeled with the policy of the image. If labeling
  fails, pack fails.

* `capture`:
  Capture an image for cloning from a running system.

## BUILD OPTIONS

* `-r`, `--addrepos` <repository>:
  Repositories to add in addition to the main source. May be specified multiple times.

* `-p`, `--packagelist` <file>:
  Filename of package list to replace default pkglist.

* `-a`, `--addpackagelist` <file>:
  A list of additional packages to include. May be specified multiple times.

* `-s`, `--source` <directory>|<image>:
  Directory to pull installation from, typically a subdirectory of `/var/lib/confluent/distributions`. By default, the repositories for the build system are used. For Ubuntu, a distribution directory is not supported, the build system repositories are always used.

  A container image made elsewhere, for example in CI, can be the source
  instead: a SIF file as built by Apptainer or SingularityCE, an OCI or
  Docker archive file as written by `podman save`, an OCI layout
  directory with its `oci-layout` file, or a podman reference with its
  transport, for example `docker://registry.example.com/hpc/compute:latest`
  or `containers-storage:localhost/compute` for a local image. Local
  images are looked up in the podman storage of the user running
  imgutil, normally root. Any other image is pulled or loaded into a
  temporary storage next to the scratch directory, which is removed
  afterwards, so the images and tags of the system stay as they are. A
  path containing a colon cannot be read by podman. A SIF file is
  extracted from its squashfs partition, which needs
  squashfs-tools 4.4 or newer but not podman.

  Any other directory or name is a distribution as above. The scratch
  directory must be empty or not exist. Missing packages are installed
  from the repositories of the image, so an image that already has every
  package builds without network access. A package list entry with a
  version, such as `curl=8.5.0-2ubuntu10`, always runs the package
  manager and needs the repositories. Units that container images mask
  are unmasked and `/usr/sbin/policy-rc.d` is removed, so the image boots
  like an installed system. Package manager settings of the image are
  kept, for example container base images leaving out documentation. The
  architecture is detected from the image. With `--arch`, the matching
  variant of an image for several architectures is used. `-r` is not supported, as the
  image brings its own repositories. EL9, Ubuntu 24.04 and SUSE 16 or
  later images are supported. With `--selinux`, the full SELinux context
  of every file, including the SELinux user, is set from the policy of
  the image.

* `--arch` <architecture>:
  Target architecture to build for (`x86_64` or `aarch64`). For Ubuntu, the
  Debian-style names `amd64` and `arm64` are accepted as aliases. Building
  for a foreign architecture is supported on EL and Ubuntu build hosts and
  requires a statically linked qemu-user emulator registered in binfmt_misc
  with the F (fix-binary) flag; on Ubuntu this is provided by the
  qemu-user-static package (qemu-user-binfmt as of Ubuntu 26.04). For EL,
  the architecture is also detected automatically when building from a `-s`
  source tree. An aarch64 image is emulated as a Neoverse N1 cpu, which needs
  qemu 7.1 or later and is much faster to emulate than the default cpu of
  qemu. To emulate another cpu, set `QEMU_CPU`, for example
  `QEMU_CPU=cortex-a72` for older qemu. The same applies to `imgutil exec`.

* `-y`, `--non-interactive`:
  Avoid prompting for confirmation.

* `--selinux`:
  Install the targeted SELinux policy and leave SELinux enforcing in the
  image instead of disabling it. EL8 and newer only. Labels come from the policy
  of the image. If labeling fails, for example because the build host
  enforces an older policy, the build fails.

* `-v`, `--volume` <mount>:
  Directory to make available in the build environment. `-v /` will cause it to be mounted in image as `/run/external/`. `-v /:/run/root` will override the target to be `/run/root`. Something like `/var/lib/repository:-` will cause it to mount to the identical path inside the image. May be specified multiple times.

* `--containerfile`:
  Write a Containerfile instead of building. It installs every package
  `build` checks for, generated from the same package lists, so building
  from the image needs no network access. Add site specific content and
  build it in CI. The OS is that of the `-s` distribution or of the build
  system, as for a build, and `--arch` names the platform, so the image
  can be built on another architecture with emulation. `-p`, `-a` and
  `--selinux` change the packages. Pass the same options to `build`
  later, as the command shown in the file does. `-r` is not supported,
  as the image installs from the repositories of its base image. EL9,
  Ubuntu 24.04 and SUSE 16 or later are supported.

* `--apptainer`:
  With `--containerfile`, write an Apptainer definition file for building
  a SIF instead.

* `--from` <image>:
  With `--containerfile`, the base image to use instead of the default for
  the OS (AlmaLinux for EL, the official Ubuntu image, openSUSE Leap for
  SUSE).

* <scratchdir>:
  Directory to build new diskless root in. With `--containerfile`, the file
  to write, or `-` for standard output.

## EXEC OPTIONS

* `-v`, `--volume` <mount>:
  Directory to make available in the build environment. `-v /` will cause it to be mounted in image as `/run/external/`. `-v /:/run/root` will override the target to be `/run/root`. May be specified multiple times.

* <scratchdir>:
  Directory of an unpacked diskless root.

* <cmd>:
  Optional command to run (defaults to a shell). Put `--` before a
  command that has options.

## UNPACK OPTIONS

* <profilename>:
  The diskless OS profile to unpack.

* <scratchdir>:
  Directory to extract diskless root to.

## PACK OPTIONS

* `-b`, `--baseprofile` <profile>:
  Profile to copy extra info from. For example, to make a new version of an existing profile, reference the previous one as baseprofile.

* `-u`, `--unencrypted`:
  Pack an unencrypted image rather than encrypting.

* <scratchdir>:
  Directory containing diskless root.

* <profilename>:
  The desired diskless OS profile name to pack the root into.

## CAPTURE OPTIONS

* <node>:
  Node to capture image from.

* <profilename>:
  Profile name for captured image.

## EXAMPLES

Build a diskless image from a distribution:

    imgutil build -s  alma-9.6-x86_64 /tmp/myimage

Build an aarch64 EL diskless image on an x86_64 host (the architecture is
detected from the source tree):

    imgutil build -s alma-9.6-aarch64 /tmp/myimage

Build an aarch64 Ubuntu diskless image on an amd64 Ubuntu host:

    imgutil build --arch aarch64 /tmp/myimage

Build from an image made from a Dockerfile and pack it:

    podman build -t localhost/myimage .
    imgutil build -s containers-storage:localhost/myimage /tmp/myimage
    imgutil pack /tmp/myimage myprofile

Build from an image that CI pushed to a registry:

    imgutil build -s docker://registry.example.com/hpc/compute:latest /tmp/myimage

Build from an Apptainer image:

    imgutil build -s /var/tmp/compute.sif /tmp/myimage

Prepare an image of the build system OS in CI that builds without network
access:

    imgutil build --containerfile Containerfile
    podman build -t localhost/compute .
    podman save -o compute.tar localhost/compute
    imgutil build -s compute.tar /tmp/compute

The same for EL10 as a SIF:

    imgutil build --containerfile --apptainer -s alma-10.0-x86_64 compute.def
    apptainer build compute.sif compute.def
    imgutil build -s compute.sif /tmp/compute

Execute a shell in an unpacked image:

    imgutil exec /tmp/myimage

Execute a specific command in an image:

    imgutil exec /tmp/myimage -- rpm -qa

Run an ansible playbook in an image through `imgutil exec` (chroot
connection of community.general):

    cat > /usr/local/sbin/imgutil-chroot << 'EOF'
    #!/bin/sh
    exec /opt/confluent/bin/imgutil exec -- "$@"
    EOF
    chmod 755 /usr/local/sbin/imgutil-chroot
    ansible-playbook -c community.general.chroot \
        -e ansible_chroot_exe=/usr/local/sbin/imgutil-chroot \
        -i /tmp/myimage, site.yml

Unpack an existing profile for modification:

    imgutil unpack myprofile /tmp/myimage

Pack a modified image into a new profile:

    imgutil pack /tmp/myimage myprofile-v2

Capture an image from a running node:

    imgutil capture node01 production-image

## FILES

* `/var/lib/confluent/public/os/`:
  Default location for OS profiles.

* `/var/lib/confluent/private/os/`:
  Location for encrypted image keys and private data.

* `/var/lib/confluent/distributions/`:
  Default location for installation sources.

## SEE ALSO

osdeploy(8)

